{"author":"RebarBotNava","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"body":"## Mechanism observed (RED), not assumed\n\nReproduced with a synthetic sentinel in a controlled child environment. The raw form renders\nthe mapping; the bool-bound form renders a bool:\n\n```\nE  AssertionError: assert 'REBAR_OPCERT_KEY_PATH' not in environ({'SHELL': ..., <SENTINEL>, ...})\nE   +  where environ({...}) = os.environ\n```\nvs.\n```\nE  assert not True\n```\n\npytest's bounded mapping repr truncates the MIDDLE, so head and tail entries render in full —\ntruncation is not mitigation.\n\n## Why the existing guard did not catch it — pattern set, not population\n\n`tests/unit/test_subprocess_env_repr_security.py` rglobs **all** of `tests/**`, so its\npopulation already covers the offending file. Its detector (`_raw_env_audit`) matches only\nenv-**construction** shapes — `dict(os.environ)`, `os.environ.copy()`, `{**os.environ}` —\nbecause its purpose is routing subprocess environments through `subprocess_env()`. An\nassert-comparator reference constructs nothing, so this is a genuinely different shape it does\nnot cover, not a population gap. Same underlying hazard class (a raw `os.environ` reaching a\nrepr), third distinct route: fixture argument reprs (`test_fixture_env_repr_security.py`),\nsubprocess env dicts (`test_subprocess_env_repr_security.py`), and now assertion operands.\n\n## Fix\n\n1. `tests/unit/test_rp04_s6_opcert_startup_heldout.py` — membership bound to a bool before the\n   assert. No other site touched; the safe `os.environ.get(...)` assertions listed in the\n   description were verified unmodified.\n2. New sibling oracle `tests/unit/test_environ_assert_repr_security.py`, pytest-resident (not a\n   CI-only step, so it is portable per `project.portability`):\n   - **AST audit** over `tests/**`: an `assert` whose test *or message* names `os.environ`\n     itself is a finding; a reference CONSUMED as the receiver of an attribute access or a\n     subscript (`os.environ.get(\"X\")`, `os.environ[\"X\"]`) is sanctioned, since only the\n     retrieved value becomes an operand. That rule flags exactly one site tree-wide (`:232`)\n     and zero of the safe forms — no churn sweep.\n   - **Behavioural probe** anchored to the REAL site: the guard's shipped statements are\n     extracted by AST from the live module (following the binding forward), executed in a\n     subprocess pytest run with the seam var set plus a sentinel, at `--tb=long` and\n     `--tb=short`. It asserts the sentinel is ABSENT from the output. A reworded assertion\n     message cannot make it pass.\n   - Detector unit tests both ways: five reintroduction shapes flagged, three value-scoped\n     shapes sanctioned.\n\n## RED -> GREEN -> mutation\n\n- RED (before fix): 3 failed, 9 passed — audit reported `...heldout.py:232`; probe leaked the\n  sentinel at BOTH traceback styles.\n- GREEN (after fix): 12 passed. Target module still 47 passed alongside both sibling oracles.\n- MUTATION (raw form reintroduced): back to 3 failed, 9 passed — audit reported `:233`, probe\n  leaked at both styles again. Restored, green.\n\n## caused_by\n\n`94188e90c0d7114ac4787b37bc8ba34d6bc9c6e4` — \"RP-04 S6: compose op-cert signer at startup\"\n(2026-08-18 08:18 UTC), which created the file and the leaking line together.\n\nDate test applied, because three of four attributions today turned out to be unmet obligations\nwhere the fix site was not the cause site. Here it is NOT an unmet obligation: the ambient-secret\npolicy already existed in the tree when the line landed — `aa41c2e45a` (\"Limit fixture\nenvironments to required keys\", 08:04 UTC) is a confirmed **ancestor** of `94188e9`\n(`git merge-base --is-ancestor`), by ~14 minutes. The obligation predated the commit, so the\ncause site is the commit that wrote the line. Fair caveat: the pre-existing oracle guarded a\ndifferent route, so no mechanism could have caught it — that mechanical gap is what this change\ncloses. The later `48d4a4d4a9` (subprocess redaction, 2026-08-18 18:41 UTC) did not create the\nobligation either; it widened a sibling route without reaching this shape.\n\n## Gates\n\n`make lint` PASS, `make typecheck` PASS (598 files),\n`check_complexity_baseline.py --check` -> `active=49 new=0 increased=0 stale=0`,\n`check_wall_clock_asserts.py` clean.\n\nNo real secret was printed at any point; the sentinel is a synthetic constant."},"env_id":"b8a354e0-12c3-4cf2-bbf1-fcc9685d5126","event_type":"COMMENT","timestamp":1787266081488599001,"uuid":"5eac404d-9d94-434e-ba57-4dbb74b7a80a"}